# Complete Operational Manual: Agentic AI for Heat-Risk Policy Analysis
## Singapore Outdoor Worker Heat-Stress Rules & Global Comparators

---

## PART A: POLICY REGISTRY — EXACT POLICIES, DATES, TERMS & LINKS

### A.1 Singapore — Primary Policy Target

| Field | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Policy Name** | Workplace Safety and Health (General Provisions) Regulations — Heat Stress Management for Outdoor Workers |
| **Enabling Act** | Workplace Safety and Health Act 2006 (Chapter 354A) |
| **Regulatory Body** | Ministry of Manpower (MOM), Singapore |
| **Advisory Body** | Workplace Safety and Health Council (WSH Council) |
| **Commencement** | 1 December (verify year: 2023 or 2024 on Singapore Statutes Online) |
| **Status** | Mandatory (verify enforcement vs. advisory phase) |
| **Key Provisions** | Risk assessment, work-rest cycles, hydration, shade, acclimatisation, training, emergency response |
| **Trigger Mechanism** | Wet-Bulb Globe Temperature (WBGT) index thresholds |
| **Affected Sectors** | Construction, shipbuilding, landscaping, cleaning, waste, utilities, road works |
| **Penalties** | Verify: composition sums, stop-work orders, prosecution under WSH Act |
| **Official Source 1** | Singapore Statutes Online: `https://sso.agc.gov.sg/Act/WSHA2006` |
| **Official Source 2** | MOM Workplace Safety: `https://www.mom.gov.sg/workplace-safety-and-health` |
| **Official Source 3** | WSH Council: `https://www.wshc.sg` |
| **Official Source 4** | MOM Newsroom: `https://www.mom.gov.sg/newsroom` |
| **Search Query** | `"heat stress" AND "outdoor workers" AND site:mom.gov.sg` |

#### Singapore — Key Terms & Definitions to Extract

| Term | Definition to Verify |
|---|---|
| Heat stress | Physiological burden from heat exposure during work |
| WBGT | Wet-Bulb Globe Temperature — composite index of heat, humidity, wind, solar radiation |
| Outdoor work | Work performed in open areas exposed to solar radiation and ambient heat |
| Work-rest cycle | Mandated ratio of labour to rest based on WBGT threshold |
| Acclimatisation | Gradual physiological adaptation to heat over 7–14 days |
| Heat illness | Spectrum: heat rash → heat cramps → heat exhaustion → heat stroke |
| Competent person | Trained supervisor responsible for monitoring heat conditions |
| Risk assessment | Employer duty to evaluate heat exposure before work commences |

---

### A.2 Global Comparator Policies — Enacted & In Force

| # | Country / Region | Policy Name | Enacted | Key Provisions | Enforcement Mechanism | Official Link / Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **California, USA** | Title 8, Section 3395 — Heat Illness Prevention Standard | 2005 (updated 2015, 2022) | Mandatory water, shade, rest breaks; high-heat procedures at 95°F; acclimatisation plan; emergency response | Cal/OSHA inspections; citations up to $25,000/violation | `https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/3395.html` |
| 2 | **Qatar** | Ministerial Decree No. 16 of 2007 — Midday Work Ban | 2007 (annual renewal) | Prohibits outdoor work 10:00–15:30, 1 June–31 August; mandates rest areas, water, first aid | Ministry of Labour inspections; site closure | Qatar Official Gazette; Ministry of Labour |
| 3 | **UAE** | Ministerial Decree No. 318 of 2005 — Midday Break | 2005 (updated annually) | Bans outdoor work 12:30–15:00, 15 June–15 September; requires shaded rest, cold water, first aid | MOHRE inspections; fines per worker | `https://www.mohre.gov.ae` |
| 4 | **Saudi Arabia** | Ministerial Decision No. 3337 — Outdoor Work Ban | 2014 | Prohibits outdoor work 12:00–15:00, 15 June–15 September | Ministry of Human Resources inspections | Saudi Official Gazette |
| 5 | **Spain** | Royal Decree 486/1997 — Workplace Environmental Conditions | 1997 | Indoor temp 17–27°C; outdoor work must account for weather; risk assessment for extreme heat | Labour Inspectorate (ITSS) | BOE: `https://www.boe.es/buscar/doc.php?id=BOE-A-1997-8896` |
| 6 | **Australia** | Model WHS Regulations — Managing Risks of Heat Stress | 2012 (model code) | Risk assessment; control measures; health monitoring; no specific national threshold | State regulators (SafeWork NSW, WorkSafe VIC, etc.) | `https://www.safeworkaustralia.gov.au` |
| 7 | **EU (Framework)** | Council Directive 89/391/EEC — OSH Framework Directive | 1989 | General employer duty to assess and mitigate risks including thermal stress | National transposition; varies by member state | `https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:31989L0391` |
| 8 | **South Korea** | Industrial Safety and Health Act — Heat Disorder Prevention Guidelines | 2019 (guidelines) | WBGT-based work-rest cycles; employer duty to provide water, shade, training | Ministry of Employment and Labor | MOEL guidelines |
| 9 | **Japan** | Ordinance on Industrial Safety and Health — Heat Illness Prevention | 2019 (revised guidelines) | WBGT reference values; employer duty; acclimatisation; buddy system | Labour Standards Inspection Offices | MHLW: `https://www.mhlw.go.jp` |
| 10 | **Bangladesh** | Bangladesh Labour Act 2006, Section 53 (general welfare) | 2006 | General ventilation/welfare; NO specific outdoor heat threshold | Department of Inspection for Factories and Establishments | `https://moil.gov.bd` |

---

### A.3 Countries with Intent but NO Binding Follow-Through

| # | Country / Body | What Was Proposed | Year | Why It Stalled | Current Status | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **USA (Federal)** | OSHA Advanced Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (ANPRM) on Heat Injury Prevention | Oct 2021 | Political opposition; industry lobbying; regulatory review delays; no final rule issued | STALLED — Only General Duty Clause applies federally | `https://www.osha.gov/heat` |
| 2 | **India** | National Heat Action Framework; Ahmedabad Heat Action Plan (2013) | 2013–2023 | No binding national law for outdoor workers; state-level plans are advisory; enforcement capacity weak | ADVISORY ONLY — No mandatory employer penalty | NDMA guidelines; Ahmedabad Municipal Corporation |
| 3 | **EU** | Calls for specific occupational heat thresholds under Framework Directive | 2003–present | Subsidiarity principle; member states resist EU-level prescriptive thresholds; Framework Directive remains generic | NO SPECIFIC EU HEAT THRESHOLD — Only general duty | EU-OSHA reports; ETUC resolutions |
| 4 | **Australia (National)** | Proposal for a national mandatory heat standard under Safe Work Australia | 2019–present | State jurisdiction fragmentation; industry opposition; model code remains guidance | NO NATIONAL MANDATE — States vary | Safe Work Australia consultation papers |
| 5 | **UK** | TUC and Labour MPs called for maximum workplace temperature law | 2013, 2018, 2022 | Government position: "existing HSE regulations sufficient"; no specific temperature threshold enacted | NO SPECIFIC HEAT LAW — HSE says "reasonable" temperature | HSE: `https://www.hse.gov.uk/temperature/` |
| 6 | **Canada (Federal)** | Canada Labour Code Part II — general safety duty; no specific heat standard | Ongoing | Provincial jurisdiction; no federal outdoor heat threshold | PROVINCIAL PATCHWORK — No federal standard | Canada Labour Code; provincial OHS bodies |
| 7 | **Pakistan** | Sindh Heatwave Action Plan; no outdoor worker protection law | 2015+ | Legislative capacity; enforcement gaps; informal economy dominance | NO BINDING LAW | NDMA Pakistan; Sindh Govt |
| 8 | **Thailand** | Ministerial Regulation on Safety in Hot Environments (proposed) | 2010s | Not enacted into enforceable standard; general OHS law applies | GENERAL DUTY ONLY | Ministry of Labour Thailand |

