## PART E: CROSS-PROJECT ARC & MARKING ALIGNMENT

### E.1 The Adversary Arc

| Project | Adversary | Human Role | Key Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 — Evidence | None (single agent) | Verify citations, approve search | Evidence Map + Source Log |
| 2 — Design | Agent critiques its own work | Check divergence, judge critique | 4 Options + Self-Attack |
| 3 — Appraisal | Permanent Red-Teamer sub-agent | Final GO/REVISE/HALT decision | Appraisal + Red-Team Report |

### E.2 Five Marking Criteria — How This Portfolio Satisfies Each

| Criterion (20%) | Evidence in This Portfolio |
|---|---|
| 1. Policy-analysis value and fit | Real Singapore 1 Dec heat policy; global comparators; live regulatory decision |
| 2. Architecture, workflow, human-approval design | Named agents, explicit gates, rights tables, orchestration logs |
| 3. Deployment, evidence provenance, source discipline | Verified citations, graded evidence, comparative policy matrix |
| 4. Testing, red-teaming, robustness, bias, failure analysis | Self-critique, permanent Red-Teamer, failure logs, drift checks |
| 5. Documentation, reproducibility, oversight, responsible AI | Full templates, disclosure statements, reproducibility packages |

### E.3 The Governing Principle

> *"The agent argues; you decide. A submission that shows the agent 
> failing and being corrected scores higher than a flawless-looking 
> transcript. Finding the failure before it reaches a decision-maker 
> is the skill."*

Every project MUST include:
- [ ] At least one visible, documented failure
- [ ] The human catching the failure
- [ ] The correction applied
- [ ] The agent's output after correction

